We examine a variational inequality problem defined by \begin{equation}\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
\text{find } x \in C
\text{ such that}
\text{ }
\phi(x)-\langle x, J_{\beta}x\rangle-\beta\langle u, J_{\beta}x\rangle\geq 0
\text{ for all } x\in C,
\text{ with } U_C
\end{array}
\right. \end{equation} We propose a perturbed algorithm, which relies on C1.5 regularized residual, as the basis of our method. The convergence of this algorithm is analyzed under the assumption that the mapping F is locally Lipschitz continuous and the solution x* is isolated, without relying on the linearized assumption. Our approach is demonstrated using traffic equilibrium problems with non-monotone demand.
